"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[86024],{686024:e=>{e.exports="# Phase C — Widget Validation\n\nDetail rules for the validate-and-repair phase: what to compare, how to classify mismatches, how to route fixes, page-level checks, and the failure-handling vocabulary.\n\n## What to compare\n\nCompare every numeric value the widget renders against the same value on the original Tableau dashboard for the same period, slice, and filter set. Numeric values surface in:\n\n- Counter / KPI widgets — the headline number.\n- Tables and crosstabs — every cell.\n- Text widgets that interpolate measures.\n- Chart axis ticks, data labels, tooltips, and totals.\n\nAll such values are expected to match exactly:\n\n| Value type                                      | Tolerance |\n| ----------------------------------------------- | --------- |\n| `SUM`, `COUNT`, `COUNT(DISTINCT)`, `MIN`, `MAX` | Exact     |\n| Ratios, percentages, averages                   | Exact     |\n| Cumulative / running totals                     | Exact     |\n\nThe migration deliberately preserves the source-table grain, so any meaningful numeric difference is a defect — not a tolerance issue.\n\nFor widgets that don't surface numeric values directly (e.g., a categorical bar chart with no labels), validate the dimensional shape: same categories, same ordering, same encoding.\n\n## Acceptable differences (record, do not flag)\n\nThese differences are cosmetic or environment-driven and should be documented in the per-widget validation note but not treated as failures or fix-routed:\n\n- **Timezone display.** Tableau may render in the workbook author's timezone; AI/BI renders in the user's session timezone.\n- **Fiscal-year offset.** Tableau workbooks can declare a fiscal-year start; AI/BI does not, so year/quarter labels may shift.\n- **Locale formatting.** Thousands separators, decimal symbols, currency placement.\n- **Null-rendering style.** Tableau may show \"Null\" / blank / a placeholder differently than AI/BI.\n\nIf you find a difference that doesn't fit one of these categories, it's a real mismatch — classify and fix it.\n\n## Classification (pick one before fixing)\n\nNumeric defects can come from either side: the widget may be binding the wrong field, wrong aggregation, or wrong filter to a channel, or the metric view itself may have a wrong formula, grain, or filter. Inspect the widget's encoding against the metric view's schema first — widget-config fixes are scoped and cheap, and only once the encoding is confirmed correct should you conclude the metric view is at fault.\n\n| Symptom                                                                                                        | Classification    | Fix path                                                                      |\n| -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Encoding references the wrong measure/dimension or wrong aggregation; chart type, sort, axis, or color wrong   | **widget-config** | Focused sub-agent dispatch scoped to this widget (`dashboard_build.md`)       |\n| Encoding is correct, but the metric view's formula, grain, or filter is wrong (or a dimension/measure missing) | **metric-view**   | Revise the metric view YAML, recreate it, then re-fetch render data           |\n| Widget renders correct values; page filter not applied or wrong scope                                          | **filter-wiring** | Focused sub-agent dispatch scoped to the page filter and its `applies-to` set |\n\nPick exactly one classification before fixing. If a widget has both a widget-config and a metric-view defect, fix the widget-config one first — it's cheaper and often resolves the apparent metric-view symptom on its own.\n\n## Applying fixes\n\n- **Resolve obvious issues directly.** When the diagnosis points clearly to a single classification and the fix is mechanical (rename a measure, swap a chart type, add a missing dimension, scope a filter), apply it and re-validate.\n- **Discuss complex failures with the user.** If the mismatch is ambiguous, if the first fix doesn't resolve it, or if the right fix isn't clear, surface the diagnosis and the proposed change to the user and decide the next step together. Don't keep poking at the same widget hoping a different attempt will land.\n- **Always re-fetch after a fix.** A change that \"looks right\" but doesn't match the source is still a failure.\n- **Re-validate downstream widgets after a metric-view edit.** Other widgets backed by the same metric view may have shifted.\n\n## Page-level checks\n\nAfter every widget on the page is validated, confirm:\n\n- Each page filter applies to the right widgets — the `applies-to` list matches the Tableau dashboard's filter scope.\n- The layout matches the original well enough to be usable. Layout differences are not validation failures unless they make a widget unreadable; record them as `expected difference` if you want them visible in the report.\n\n## When validation can't happen\n\nPhase C requires (a) the dashboard widget render-data tool, (b) the user on the AI/BI page, and (c) a Tableau screenshot. If any of these is missing:\n\n| Missing              | Behavior                                                                                                                              |\n| -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Render-data tool     | Phase C is unavailable — surface to the user and stop. Do not invent a substitute path.                                               |\n| User not on the page | Ask the user to navigate to the AI/BI page; pause until they confirm.                                                                 |\n| Tableau screenshot   | Validate from JSON render data only. Mark each widget as `visual confirmation skipped` in the report and rely on numeric match alone. |\n| Tableau access lost  | Skip Phase C for that page. Document the page as `validation skipped — no source available` in the final report.                      |\n\n## Self-discipline reminders\n\n- **Don't rationalize mismatches.** A 5% gap is not \"rounding\" — investigate. The migration target is exact match for the value types listed above.\n- **Don't classify until you've checked.** Look at the metric view's measure formula and the widget's encoding before deciding which fix path to take. A misclassified fix wastes the user's time.\n- **Don't expand the scope.** If a widget validates, move on — don't poke at unrelated widgets while you're in there.\n"}}]);
